!Core.DateAndTime methodsFor!

asDATE
	"Answer the OLE Automation <DATE> that is the nearest equivalent of the receiver. Note that this may to lose some precision, although we preserve as much as possible. OLE Automation APIs that work with dates may discard (e.g. SystemTimeToVariantTime) or round (e.g. VariantTimeToSystemTime) sub-second precision, and this behaviour may not be documented. The receiver can also be out of range of dates that are representable as a DATE, in which case an error will be thrown from DATE class>>fromFloat:"

	<namespace: OS.COM>
	| local day fraction |
	local := self asLocal.	"DATEs are assumed to be in local time"
	day := local rataDie - DATE.EpochRD.
	fraction := local seconds / Duration.SecondsPerDay.
	^DATE fromFloat: (day + (fraction * day signum)) asFloat!

initializeVariant: anOleVariant
	self asFILETIME initializeVariant: anOleVariant! !

!Core.DateAndTime categoriesForMethods!
asDATE!converting!public! !
initializeVariant:!converting!private! !
!

!Core.DateAndTime class methodsFor!

fromDATE: aDATE
	"Answer a new instance of the receiver that represents the moment as the <DATE> (variant time) argument."

	"From [VariantTimeToSystemTime function (oleauto.h)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/oleauto/nf-oleauto-varianttimetosystemtime):

		A variant time is stored as an 8-byte real value (double), representing a date between January 1, 100 and December 31, 9999, inclusive. The value 2.0 represents January 1, 1900; 3.0 represents January 2, 1900, and so on. Adding 1 to the value increments the date by a day. The fractional part of the value represents the time of day. Therefore, 2.5 represents noon on January 1, 1900; 3.25 represents 6:00 A.M. on January 2, 1900, and so on. Negative numbers represent the dates prior to December 30, 1899.

	The conversion is relatively simple - the integer part represents a day number offset from 30/12/1899 that we just need to adjust to the Rata Die epoch. The fraction part can then be multiplied by the number of seconds in a day to yield the 
	There is an important subtlety here that is not immediately apparent: The fraction part always represents a positive offset into the day, so it doesn't necessary have the same sign as the day count. This is odd, and appears to create some complexity in conversions in either direction, but in this case it just boils down to using the absolute value of the fraction to calculate the seconds. It can only be this simple because we retain the full precision - if we were to round the value, the logic would have to be more complex to handle the asymmetry. See also DateAndTime>>#asDATE for the reverse conversion, where we do unfortunately need a branch to handle it."

	<namespace: OS.COM>
	| date day |
	date := aDATE asFloat.
	(date > DATE.LowerBound and: [date < DATE.UpperBound])
		ifFalse: [^HRESULTError signalWith: E_INVALIDARG].
	day := date truncated.
	^self
		rataDie: day + DATE.EpochRD
		seconds: (date - day) abs * Duration.SecondsPerDay
		offsetSeconds: Locale timeZoneInformation offsetSeconds! !

!Core.DateAndTime class categoriesForMethods!
fromDATE:!accessing!public! !
