!OS.FILETIME methodsFor!

asDATE
	"Answer the OLE Automation <DATE> that is the nearest equivalent of the receiver. Note that this is likely to lose some precision. FILETIMEs beyond the year 9999 are not representable as DATEs and will generate an error. DATE can represent the earliest possible FILETIME, however."

	^self asDateAndTime asDATE!

initializeVariant: anOleVariant
	anOleVariant filetime: self! !

!OS.FILETIME categoriesForMethods!
asDATE!converting!public! !
initializeVariant:!converting!private! !
!

!OS.FILETIME class methodsFor!

newSAFEARRAY: anInteger
	"Private - Answer a new SAFEARRAY initialized to hold <integer>, anInteger, DATE values."

	"The SAFEARRAY will be of type VT_DATE, even though that loses precision, because this is consistent with the behaviour of the Win32 InitVariantFromFileTimeArray API. If you want to preserve the original format, use e.g. `SAFEARRAY length: <anInteger> recordClass: FILETIME` to create a SAFEARRAY holding FILETIMEs as VT_RECORDs."

	<namespace: OS.COM.OleVariant>
	^SAFEARRAY
		length: anInteger
		vt: VT_DATE
		elementClass: DATE!

vt
	"Answer the VARTYPE (one of the VT_XXX constants) used to describe the receiver's type when represented in a <VARIANT>."

	^COM.VARIANT.VT_FILETIME! !

!OS.FILETIME class categoriesForMethods!
