!OS.SYSTEMTIME methodsFor!

asDATE
	"Answer the OLE `DATE` representation of the receiver. Note that any milliseconds are rounded rather than preserved in the fractional part of the ole date."

	<namespace: OS.COM>
	| vtime |
	vtime := DATE newBuffer.
	(OleAut32 systemTimeToVariantTime: self pvtime: vtime) ifFalse: [InvalidArgumentError signal].
	^vtime!

asFloat
	"Answer a `Float` value that is the equivalent to the OLE `DATE` representation of the receiver."

	^self asDATE asFloat!

initializeVariant: anOleVariant
	anOleVariant date: self asDATE! !

!OS.SYSTEMTIME categoriesForMethods!
asDATE!converting!public! !
asFloat!converting!public! !
initializeVariant:!converting!private! !
!

!OS.SYSTEMTIME class methodsFor!

fromDATE: aDATE
	"Answer a new instance of the receiver representing approximately the same moment as the receiver. The ole DATE representation is a double precision floating point number where the integer part represents a day offset from 30/12/1899, and the fractional part is the fraction of the day. Note that the fractional part always specifies a positive fraction of the day, regardless of whether the date is negative. In other words DATE fromFloat: -2.25 specifies 1899-12-28T06:00:00, and not 1899-12-27T18:00:00. Also note that the VariantTimeToSystemTime API will round (not discard) any fractional seconds, which is not consistent with the reverse translation SystemTimeToVariantTime, as that discards the milliseconds of the SYSTEMTIME."

	<namespace: OS.COM>
	| systime |
	systime := self new.
	(OleAut32 variantTimeToSystemTime: aDATE asFloat lpSystemTime: systime)
		ifFalse: [HRESULTError signalWith: E_FAIL].
	^systime! !

!OS.SYSTEMTIME class categoriesForMethods!
fromDATE:!public! !
!

